# Chapter 31: JSON & Serialization

Data interchange is the lifeblood of networked systems. JSON remains the lingua franca despite its verbosity, and binary formats like Protocol Buffers or CBOR squeeze every cycle out of constrained links. Zig’s approach to serialization is a perfect microcosm of its philosophy: **no hidden allocations, no runtime reflection magic, and comptime-driven code generation that feels like a natural extension of the language rather than a separate DSL.** In this chapter, we will not merely learn `std.json`; we will dissect how Zig builds a zero‑surprise parser, a streaming tokenizer, and a compile‑time serializer that can target any format you dare to design.

---

### 1. Basic Usage

#### DOM‑style Parsing

The simplest way to read JSON is to parse the entire document into a tree of `std.json.Value` nodes. You provide an allocator because the tree may contain strings, arrays, and objects that require heap memory.

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const json = std.json;

pub fn main() !void {
    var gpa = std.heap.GeneralPurposeAllocator(.{}){};
    defer _ = gpa.deinit();
    const allocator = gpa.allocator();

    const input =
        \\{
        \\  "name": "Zig",
        \\  "version": 0.13,
        \\  "tags": ["systems", "performance"]
        \\}
    ;

    // parseFromSlice returns a Parsed wrapper that owns the tree.
    var parsed = try json.parseFromSlice(
        json.Value,
        allocator,
        input,
        .{},
    );
    defer parsed.deinit();

    // Access the root value.
    const root = parsed.value; // json.Value

    // Value is a tagged union; use a switch or direct field access with .object.
    const name = root.object.get("name").?.string;
    const version = root.object.get("version").?.float;
    std.debug.print("{s} {d}\n", .{ name, @as(u8, @intFromFloat(version)) });
}
```

Every `get` returns a `?Value`, forcing you to confront missing keys. The `.object` field is a `std.StringHashMapUnmanaged(Value)`, so iteration and lookup are explicit.

#### Streaming (Token‑Based) Parsing

When data size exceeds available memory, or when you want incremental processing, use `std.json.Scanner` to pull tokens from a `std.io.Reader`. This avoids constructing an intermediate DOM entirely.

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const json = std.json;

pub fn main() !void {
    var gpa = std.heap.GeneralPurposeAllocator(.{}){};
    defer _ = gpa.deinit();
    const allocator = gpa.allocator();

    // A stream that yields JSON tokens.
    const input = "[1, 2, 3]";
    var stream = std.io.fixedBufferStream(input);
    var scanner = json.Scanner.init(stream.reader(), .{});

    // Iterate over tokens.
    while (true) {
        const token = try scanner.next();
        switch (token) {
            .array_begin => std.debug.print("array start\n", .{}),
            .array_end   => break,
            .number      => std.debug.print("number: {s}\n", .{token.number.slice}),
            else => {},
        }
    }
}
```

Tokens include `.object_begin`, `.string`, `.true`, `.null`, etc. You maintain your own state machine atop the scanner; the library does not coerce the stream into any particular data structure.

#### Stringify (Serialization)

Writing JSON is as straightforward as reading. Use `std.json.stringify` for one‑shot serialization of a known value, or `std.json.writeStream` to output to a `Writer`.

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const json = std.json;

pub fn main() !void {
    var gpa = std.heap.GeneralPurposeAllocator(.{}){};
    defer _ = gpa.deinit();
    const allocator = gpa.allocator();

    const value = json.Value{
        .object = json.ObjectMap.init(allocator),
    };
    // Fill the object (omitted for brevity).

    var buf: [1024]u8 = undefined;
    var stream = std.io.fixedBufferStream(&buf);
    try json.stringify(value, .{}, stream.writer());
    std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{stream.getWritten()});
}
```

For custom types, you will implement `jsonStringify` (see Section 8).

